WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
A phosphate just landed on a protein.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.900
Which enzyme put it there?

3
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:12.780
Your genome holds 518 protein kinases, enzymes that attach phosphate to proteins.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.800
In a living cell, many work at once, so you cannot see who did it.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.220
Isolate one suspect outside the cell.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.720
An in vitro kinase assay mixes purified kinase, a substrate, and ATP with a labelled

7
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.820
end phosphate. The kinase moves that end, or gamma, phosphate onto serine,

8
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.280
threonine, or tyrosine.

9
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.540
Near pH 7 the phosphate adds close to two negative charges.

10
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:47.003
Next, detect the transferred phosphate.

11
00:00:48.543 --> 00:00:50.463
Track its label directly on the substrate.

12
00:00:51.603 --> 00:00:56.683
Or use a phosphospecific antibody, which binds only when one site carries phosphate.

13
00:00:58.023 --> 00:01:03.183
To find unexpected sites, the enzyme trypsin cuts the protein into short pieces

14
00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:09.263
called peptides. Mass spectrometry weighs each one closely enough that one phosphate

15
00:01:09.263 --> 00:01:10.263
shows.

16
00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:15.137
Suppose a signal arrives, and your antibody band turns much darker.

17
00:01:16.417 --> 00:01:19.117
Does that prove more substrate molecules carry phosphate?

18
00:01:20.557 --> 00:01:22.957
Think of two other ways that band could darken.

19
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:32.617
First, the sample may hold more total protein, not more phosphate.

20
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:36.237
Blot the same samples for whole protein too.

21
00:01:37.877 --> 00:01:42.737
Second, an antibody can bind the wrong protein, so run a control missing the protein

22
00:01:42.737 --> 00:01:49.777
or phosphate. With those checks, a purified kinase plus labelled ATP shows what it

23
00:01:49.777 --> 00:01:51.017
can phosphorylate directly.

24
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.580
Three limits remain.

25
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.980
An antibody misses unsuspected sites and never shows what fraction of molecules

26
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:06.460
carry phosphate. Sites predicted by tools like Scansite stay guesses until someone

27
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.460
finds the phosphate.

28
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.720
And a cell-free mix lacks phosphatases, and its kinase may mark targets it never

29
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.980
reaches inside cells.

30
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.480
The assay unlocked major discoveries.

31
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:25.720
At the end of the 1970s, Cohen's lab at Vanderbilt University used membranes from

32
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.520
A-431 tumor cells.

33
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.980
They are rich in receptors for the growth factor EGF.

34
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:38.860
With Graham Carpenter doing much of the biochemistry, adding EGF sped phosphate onto

35
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.840
protein. Hiroshi Ushiro then found it landed on tyrosine.

36
00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:51.343
In 1998, De Koninck and Schulman tested purified CaM kinase II, which calcium

37
00:02:51.343 --> 00:02:54.603
switches on through the calcium-binding protein calmodulin.

38
00:02:55.923 --> 00:03:01.083
When two neighbouring subunits hold calmodulin, one can phosphorylate the other, so

39
00:03:01.083 --> 00:03:03.763
activity can persist after calcium falls.

40
00:03:05.123 --> 00:03:08.623
They showed that lasting activity depends on pulse frequency.

41
00:03:09.817 --> 00:03:16.217
Around 1990, a drug for one kinase's ATP pocket was expected to jam dozens.

42
00:03:17.557 --> 00:03:19.597
That pocket looks alike across kinases.

43
00:03:21.017 --> 00:03:26.037
In Basel, a group including Juerg Zimmermann added one methyl group, removing

44
00:03:26.037 --> 00:03:32.357
activity against protein kinase C. It still blocked ABL, and the compound became

45
00:03:32.357 --> 00:03:37.217
imatinib. One purified kinase shows what it can do directly.
